\rev{We therefore benchmarked four representation families for unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation (sinus rhythm versus atrial fibrillation), with the controls the original project lacked. The ordering of the representations follows from the class definitions and is not the contribution. SR and AF differ by definition in the regularity of the rhythm, so it is expected that compact hand-crafted features and a large foundation model both recover the split. The benchmark adds what its controls reveal and how the representations behave under deployment stress. The contributions are empirical:}
\begin{revblock}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A clustering benchmark of raw, convolutional autoencoder (conv-AE), hand-crafted and two frozen foundation-model representations. It is chance-corrected and significance-tested, and it is patient-grouped: every record of a patient stays in one fold. A supervised classifier trained on each representation sets its supervised ceiling, against which its clustering is read. The ordering is shown stable across the assumed number of clusters.
\item Two reproducible methodological cautions that explain the original project's misleading numbers. Internal validity indices, which score a partition from the data alone without labels, reward the degenerate one-cluster solution. Feature scaling, the per-feature transformation applied before clustering, changes the result by a wide margin.
\item A deployment comparison under two independent stressors: heavy class imbalance, and federation across sites that differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence. The 16 hand-crafted features overtake ECGFounder, the stronger foundation model on balanced data, under imbalance. Across the federated sites they keep a larger share of the adjusted Rand index (ARI) they reach without federation than either foundation model does.
\item A generalization check on a MIT-BIH beat-morphology task, the data\-set the closest prior benchmark omits.
\end{enumerate}
\end{revblock}
\rev{We propose no new method, and we credit the separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon to prior work. Every statement about a foundation model is scoped to the two models tested, for the reasons given in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.}

